## Liver metastasis

- **Definition** - metastatic lesions from a distant primary tumour, and are the most common malignant tumours of the liver
- **Etiology of liver metastasis** - primary GIT tumours (haematogenous spread from portal venous system):
  - <u>Metastatic colorectal carinoma</u> - most relevant because well-documented long term survival after liver resection (considered currative)
  - <u>Upper GI carcinoma</u> - esophageal or gastric carcinoma
  - <u>Other HBP tumours</u> - pancreatic, biliary
  - <u>Urogenital tumours</u> - primary renal tumours, prostate tumours
  - <u>Melaenoma</u> - eye and skin
  - <u>Gynaecological tumours</u> - ovarian, endometrial cervical
  - <u>Soft tissue tumours</u> - retroperitoneal sarcoma
- **Liver metastasis can be currative:**
  - Metastatic colorectal carcinoma - extensively studied and documented potential for long-term survival and cure
  - Other liver metastasis - can be potentially curative by hepatic resection, but data is less compelling than for colorectal cancer

### Hepatic metastasis of primary colorectal carcinoma

- **Epidemiology** - 60% of patients w/ CRC will have hepatic metastasis during their disease course:
  - **Time-course:**
    - <u>Synchronous liver metastasis</u> - present at time of diagnosis of primary disease (worse prognosis)
    - <u>Metachronous liver metastasis</u> - arbitrarily defined as \> 1y after diagnosis of primary disease
  - **Potential for curative resection:**
    - <u>Candidates for curative hepatic resection</u> - 5-10% initially, but up to 20% through downstaging by neoadjuvant chemotherapy
    - <u>Non-candidates for curative hepatic resection</u> - most patients with widespread metastasis of extensive unresectable hepatic metastasis
- **Clinical presentation of colorectal metastasis:**
  - <u>Symptomatic presentation</u> (rare currently, and often unresectable) - e.g. peritoneal carcinomatosis, malignant ascites, weight loss, palpable mass
  - <u>Presentation of synchronous tumours</u> - identified through staging Ix at time of Dx (e.g. Contrast CT T+A+P, diagnostic laparotomy)
  - <u>Presentation of metachronous tumours</u> - through routine follow-up for resected primary CRC with liver USG, LFT, and CEA (no trials indicating higher chance of resectability and improved survival, but only through clinical operations)
- **Ix:**
  - **LFT** - dLFT with elevation of ALP, GGT and LDH
  - **Imaging:**
    - **Contrast, helical HRCT with liver protocol A+P:**
      - Assessment of resectability and pre-operative planning - characterisation of lesion particularly during portal venous phase to maximize hepatic parenchymal enhancement
      - Detection of extrahepatic disease
    - +/- **PET-CT** - added benefit of detecting occult extrahepatic disease (25% have change in Mx)
  - **Colonoscopy** - detect local recurrence and presence of metachronous colorectal lesion
- **Mx:**
  - **Partial hepetectomy** - if resectable liver metastasis
  - **Adjuvant chemotherapy** - general consensus of 6mo 5-FU-based chemotherapy (evidence is mixed)

