## Principles of Hepatic recection

- **Historical perspective** - modern advances in major hepatic surgery dramatically improves outcomes:
  - **Improved outcomes:**
    - <u>Outcomes in 1970s-1980s</u> - high operative mortality (10-20%) owing to incomplete understanding of liver vascular haemodynamics causing massive intraoperative blood loss
    - <u>Outcomes in modern experienced centres</u> - \<= 5% due to reduced blood loss
  - **Reasons for improved perioperative outcomes:**
    - <u>Better understanding of blood loss</u> - intraoperative blood loss mainly comes from hepatic veins, such that surgeons now perform hepatic resections in low CVP conditions (e.g. CVP monitoring, Trendelenberg position, venodilators, and fluid restriction)
    - <u>Better understanding of segmental liver anatomy</u> - improved techniques to transect liver tissue and control bleeders primarily due to improved understanding of liver vascular anatomy
- **Factors affecting perioperative morbidity of hepatic resections:**
  - Blood loss
  - Amount of normal liver resected
  - Condition of the liveer (e.g. cirrhosis)
- **Complications of liver resection:**
- **Techniques of liver resection** - differ according to the disease being treated:
  - <u>Benign liver disease</u> - enucleation indicated based on symptoms or infection
  - <u>Malignant liver disease</u> (except for colorectal liver metastasis) - formal anatomical resection (disease free margins) in most cases, wedge resection (higher risk of margin involvement) are used sparingly
- **Nomenclature for most common major anatomic hepatic resections:**
  - <u>Right hepatectomy</u> (or right hemihepatectomy) - resection of segments V through VIII
  - <u>Left hepatectomy</u> (or left hemihepatectomy) - resection of segments II through IV
  - <u>Extended right-left hepatectomy</u> (or right trisectionectomy) - resection of segments IV through VIII
  - <u>Extended left-right hepatectomy</u> (or left trisectionectomy) - resection of segments II through V, and VIII
  - <u>Left lateral sectionectomy</u> - resection of segments II to III

